*
* START-HISTORY:
* 18 Oct 26 Windows port - from Linux 3bca321 (12 Oct 26): The name now gives a one-character style CODE (the shadow keeps one per cell), and
*               $sl_sgr turns a code into the sequence to DISPLAY; before, the name gave the sequence.
* 18 Oct 26 Windows port - from Linux 7ff476f (12 Oct 26): The table is $sl_theme's: the codes are the same in every theme and the theme says what
*               each looks like (colours as well as reverse video and underline).
* 18 Oct 26 Windows port - from Linux c798144 (12 Oct 26): New.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   call $sl_attr(name, code)
*
*     name  a style: normal, reverse, underline, both, bold, window, highlight, bar, barsel, hotkey,
*           hotkey.bar, field, disabled, desktop (what each is for, and its code, are in $sl_theme), or a
*           code itself
*     code  returned: the style's code (sl.h); n for an empty name or one it does not know
*
*   What a code looks like is the theme's business ($sl_theme, $sl_sgr), so a widget names the style it
*   wants and never a colour.  In the default theme every style is reverse video, underline, both or
*   plain, so it reads on a monochrome terminal.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine sl_attr(name, code)
$catalog $sl_attr

   call $sl_theme('code', name, '', '', '', code)

   return
end

* END-CODE
